🧠 What This File Is Doing

This file creates a global authentication system using React Context.

Instead of fetching /api/user in every component, you:

Fetch once

Store user in global state

Access it anywhere

This uses the Context API from React.

🔹 Step 1 — Create the Context
const AuthContext = createContext(null);

Think of this as:

"Create a global box where auth data will live."

Right now it’s empty (null).

🔹 Step 2 — AuthProvider Component
export function AuthProvider({ children }) {

This component wraps your entire app:

<AuthProvider>
  <App />
</AuthProvider>

Everything inside it can access auth data.

🔹 Step 3 — State
const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
user

null → not logged in

{ username: "bob", ... } → logged in

loading

true while checking login status

false once we know

`🔹 Step 4 — Auto-Fetch User on App Load
useEffect(() => {
  fetch("/api/user")
    .then(res => res.json())
    .then(data => {
      if (data.success) setUser(data.userinfo);
    })
    .finally(() => setLoading(false));
}, []);`

This runs once when the app starts.

What happens:

App loads

It calls /api/user

If user is logged in (cookie/session exists) → backend returns user

setUser(...) updates global state

loading becomes false

Now your whole app knows whether someone is logged in.

🔹 Step 5 — Login Function
const login = async (credentials) => {
  const res = await fetch("/api/login", { method: "POST", body: JSON.stringify(credentials) });
  const data = await res.json();
  if (data.success) {
    setUser(data.userinfo);
  }
};

When user logs in:

Send credentials to backend

Backend sets session / cookie

Backend returns user info

setUser(...) updates global state

Navbar updates automatically

No reload needed.

🔹 Step 6 — Logout Function
const logout = async () => {
  await fetch("/api/logout");
  setUser(null);
};

When logging out:

Backend clears session

setUser(null) removes user

Entire app re-renders

Protected UI disappears

Again — no reload.

🔹 Step 7 — Provide the Data Globally
<AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, login, logout, loading }}>
  {children}
</AuthContext.Provider>

This is the key line.

You are saying:

"Everything inside this provider can access user, login, logout, and loading."

🔹 Step 8 — Custom Hook
export const useAuth = () => useContext(AuthContext);

Instead of writing:

useContext(AuthContext)

everywhere, you can just write:

const { user, logout } = useAuth();

Much cleaner.

🧠 What Happens When State Changes?

If you call:

setUser(null)

React automatically:

Re-renders Navbar

Re-renders protected routes

Updates UI everywhere

That’s the power of centralized state.

